Chapter 7 – The Birth of the 'Tech Rider' Part 1
Curtis stepped into the dealership looking like a different person.
The bloodied white shirt and ruined school uniform were gone. In their place was the structured gray-and-red jacket Justice had left him, white shirt, red tie, black pants, red Jumpman cap, and the gold-framed Louis Vuitton sunglasses that hid his mismatched eyes. The long black braids fell neatly down his chest. He no longer looked like someone who had walked out of a street fight. He looked like someone who could put a Harley on a card without blinking.
The same salesman from earlier was behind the counter.
The man’s expression went through a rapid, visible adjustment. The frown he had worn when Curtis was bloodstained and rough around the edges disappeared, replaced by the polished, eager smile of someone who had just watched a potential high-ticket customer walk through the door.
He came out from behind the counter immediately.
“Welcome in,” the salesman said, voice warmer than it had been the first time. “Looking for something specific today, or just browsing?”
His eyes flicked once over the jacket, the sunglasses, the quiet confidence in the way Curtis carried himself. Whatever calculation he was making, it had clearly landed on the profitable side.
Curtis came to a stop near the row of imported Harleys, the same ones that had caught his attention earlier, and regarded the salesman over the top of the gold frames.
The difference in treatment was not lost on him.
Curtis inclined his head slightly toward the salesman, the gold-framed sunglasses catching the showroom lights.
“Hello. My apologies for my improper appearance earlier. I hope I did not upset you.”
The salesman blinked once, then recovered smoothly, the professional smile widening.
“Not at all, not at all. Happens more often than you’d think—people come in straight from work or, ah, unexpected situations.” He gave a light, diplomatic laugh. “You look much more prepared this time. Are you interested in any of the imports in particular? The Harleys on that side are all genuine U.S. models. We don’t get many of them in.”
He gestured toward the exact row Curtis had been staring at through the window earlier, already shifting into full sales mode now that the customer in front of him looked like someone with both taste and money.
Curtis followed the gesture with a slight turn of his head, the long black braids shifting against the gray-and-red jacket. Behind the reflective lenses, his expression remained unreadable, but he didn’t correct the salesman’s assumptions.
Curtis’s gaze moved along the row of imported Harleys—and stopped.
The red one sat slightly apart from the others, low and aggressive, the kind of deep crimson that looked almost wet under the showroom lights. Black wheels. Black exhaust. Clean lines. It didn’t try to be subtle. It looked like it had been built to be noticed and then outrun whatever noticed it.
The salesman followed his eyes and immediately stepped closer, professional enthusiasm rising.
“Ah. You’ve got good taste. That’s a Softail Standard with a full custom package—Vance & Hines exhaust, upgraded wheels, the red is a limited factory color matched with blacked-out hardware. Milwaukee-Eight 107 engine. It’s got torque for days and still handles cleaner than most of the heavier cruisers. One of the few genuine U.S. imports we have left on the floor.”
He rested a hand lightly on the seat, careful not to smudge anything.
“We don’t see many people walk in and zero in on this one so fast. Most customers need the full tour first.” A small, hopeful smile. “Would you like to hear the numbers, or would you rather sit on it?”
Curtis remained still for a moment, gold-framed sunglasses reflecting the red of the tank. Behind the lenses, something quieter and more personal than simple interest had settled in his expression.
The jacket Justice had left him felt heavier across his shoulders.
He didn’t answer right away.
Curtis let out a low, quiet chuckle behind the gold-framed sunglasses.
“Let’s sit down.” He nodded once toward the red Softail. “I already made my decision.”
The salesman blinked, then smiled wider—the particular smile of a man who had just realized the sale was already over and all that remained was paperwork.
“Of course. Right this way.”
He led Curtis away from the display floor toward a clean glass-walled office at the side of the showroom, already pulling a folder and a tablet as they walked. The red Harley remained visible through the glass, chrome and crimson catching the lights like it was waiting.
Curtis took the offered seat without removing the sunglasses or the red Jumpman cap. The structured gray-and-red jacket settled around him as he leaned back slightly, long black braids falling forward over one shoulder. He looked completely at ease—like buying an imported motorcycle in cash was simply the next logical step in his evening rather than a significant purchase.
The salesman sat across from him, still slightly surprised by how quickly the decision had been made, and opened the folder.
“Excellent choice. Let’s get the numbers in front of you.”
Curtis didn’t wait for the full price breakdown.
He looked across the desk at the salesman, gold-framed sunglasses still in place, and asked the only question that mattered to him.
“Do you take straight cash?”
Before the man could fully answer, Curtis reached down, lifted the duffle bag onto the desk, and unzipped it in one smooth motion. Neat, dense stacks of currency sat inside—enough to cover the red Softail several times over without touching the rest of what he had left at the flat.
The salesman’s professional smile froze for half a second.
His eyes moved from the open bag to Curtis’s calm expression, then back to the money. Whatever internal script he usually followed for financing options and credit checks quietly died on the spot.
“…Yes,” he said carefully, voice a little higher than before. “We can take cash. Of course. I’ll just need to… process a few things. Verify. Standard procedure with a purchase of this size.”
He was already reaching for the phone on the desk, though his eyes kept drifting back to the open duffle bag like he was still confirming it was real.
Curtis leaned back in the chair, long black braids shifting against the gray-and-red jacket, and waited without further comment. The red Harley sat visible through the glass behind the salesman, patient and already claimed.
Curtis gave a small, easy nod at the salesman’s caution.
“That’s fine. It’s not acquired by illegal means. But I completely understand.”
He left the duffle bag open on the desk and simply waited, gold-framed sunglasses reflecting the office lights, expression calm and unbothered. No pressure. No impatience. Just a man who had already decided the red Softail was leaving with him and was willing to let the dealership do whatever verification they needed.
The salesman made his calls. Papers were checked. The cash was counted twice. Whatever internal flags a full cash purchase of an imported Harley might have raised were quietly cleared. Twenty minutes later the man returned looking noticeably more relaxed, a set of keys and a folder of documents in hand.
“Everything’s in order,” he said, smiling with genuine relief this time. “Congratulations. She’s all yours.”
Curtis zipped the duffle bag closed, accepted the keys, and stood. The structured gray-and-red jacket settled across his shoulders as he looked through the glass at the red Softail waiting on the showroom floor.
For the first time since arriving in Ikebukuro, something felt straightforward.
He had a bike.
Curtis had the keys in hand and was already turning toward the red Softail when something else caught his eye.
On a high glass shelf near the accessories wall sat a helmet that looked like it belonged in a different decade—or a different world. Sleek black shell. White angular accents that rose like stylized horns or wings. A glowing circular blue visor at the center. Long synthetic braids in shades of blue and gray hung from the base, shifting slightly under the showroom lights.
Curtis stopped.
He pointed.
“What’s that?! And does it come in red?”
The salesman’s eyes lit up with the particular gleam of a man who had just watched a cash buyer notice the most expensive display piece in the store.
“Ah— that. Limited import. Full custom smart helmet, adaptive visor, reinforced composite shell, integrated comms. The braids are modular. And yes…” He reached under the counter and brought out a second unit still in its case. “It comes in crimson.”
He set the red version on the counter between them. The same aggressive, futuristic design, only now the shell matched the Softail almost perfectly.
Curtis stared at it for a long second behind the gold-framed sunglasses.
Then he nodded once.
“I’ll take it.”
The salesman looked like he might actually cry from joy.
By the time Curtis walked out of the dealership, he owned both the red Harley-Davidson Softail and one of the most expensive helmets on the floor. The duffle bag was lighter. The keys were heavy in his hand. And for the first time since arriving in Ikebukuro, he had something that was purely, uncomplicatedly his.
